{#
  T-2.11 — the confirmation dialog for a destructive action.

  Usage:

    {% set policy %}
        <a href="{{ url }}" class="dialog__link">…</a>
    {% endset %}

    {% include 'components.ui.dialog' with {
        id:            'cancel-order-1042',
        title:         trans('…'),
        message:       trans('…'),
        confirm_label: trans('…'),
        extra:         policy
    } only %}

    <button type="button" data-sheet-open="cancel-order-1042">…</button>

  Parameters:
    id             required — unique on the page; the trigger names it
    title          required — the dialog's accessible name and its heading
    message        required — the consequence, in plain words. Through |trans.
    confirm_label  required — names the action, never «نعم». Through |trans.
    dismiss_label  defaults to «إغلاق», which is what the artboard draws
    confirm_variant 'error' (default) · any variant of components.ui.button
    confirm_href   optional — renders the confirm control as a link
    extra          optional captured markup under the message; the artboard's
                   «سياسة الإلغاء» link is this
    level          heading level for the title, 2 by default

  IT ADDS NO OVERLAY BEHAVIOUR, AND THAT IS THE POINT. Focus in, focus trapped,
  focus returned, Esc, backdrop click and the scroll lock are all T-2.10's, which
  in turn are the browser's `<dialog>`. This file is the arrangement the artboard
  draws inside it: a message, and two actions side by side.

  «THE DESTRUCTIVE ACTION IS NOT THE DEFAULT FOCUS TARGET» IS ALREADY TRUE, AND
  NOTHING HERE ARRANGES IT. T-2.10 puts `autofocus` on the close button, so focus
  opens there; the footer follows the body in the DOM, and within the footer the
  dismiss comes first. The confirm is therefore the last thing a keyboard reaches
  — which is also the artboard's own order, «إغلاق» at the inline start and
  «تأكيد الإلغاء» at the inline end. Reading order and safety agree here, so
  neither had to be traded for the other.

  THE CONFIRM CARRIES A HOOK, NOT A HANDLER. `data-dialog-confirm="{{ id }}"` is
  a stable selector and nothing more: this component cannot know whether the
  caller cancels an order over the API, submits a form, or follows a link, and
  guessing would put business logic in a design-system primitive. A caller either
  passes `confirm_href`, or binds to `[data-dialog-confirm="…"]` in its own
  script. **It also does not close the dialog on confirm**, because a caller that
  must show progress before the request resolves needs it open.
#}
{% set dialog_confirm_variant = confirm_variant|default('error') %}

{% set dialog_body %}
    <p class="dialog__message">{{ message }}</p>

    {% if extra is defined and extra is not empty %}
        <div class="dialog__extra">{{ extra }}</div>
    {% endif %}
{% endset %}

{% set dialog_footer %}
    {#
      Dismiss first in the DOM: it is the inline-start button in the artboard,
      and it is the safe one. `data-sheet-close` is the primitive's own hook.
    #}
    {% include 'components.ui.button' with {
        label:   dismiss_label|default(trans('theme.common.close')),
        variant: 'neutral',
        class:   'dialog__action',
        attrs:   { 'data-sheet-close': '' }
    } only %}

    {% include 'components.ui.button' with {
        label:   confirm_label,
        variant: dialog_confirm_variant,
        href:    confirm_href|default(''),
        class:   'dialog__action',
        attrs:   { 'data-dialog-confirm': id }
    } only %}
{% endset %}

{% include 'components.ui.bottom-sheet' with {
    id:      id,
    title:   title,
    level:   level|default(2),
    variant: 'dialog',
    class:   'dialog',
    body:    dialog_body,
    footer:  dialog_footer
} only %}
